  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 1,
    "lesson_title": "Convolution Theorem",
    "lesson_core_move": "State the convolution theorem as the operational equivalence between continuous-time convolution and Fourier-domain multiplication, connecting direct LTI output convolution to frequency-domain signal analysis.",
    "section_count": 4,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Convolution Theorem",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Convolution Theorem",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "When an LTI output is written as $y(t)=x(t)*h(t)$, the convolution theorem tells you how to read its Fourier-domain form: the output spectrum is the product $Y(j\\omega)=X(j\\omega)H(j\\omega)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=x(t)*h(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the same system described two ways. On the time-domain side, the input and impulse response combine by convolution; on the frequency-domain side, their spectra combine by multiplication. The theorem gives the matching between those two descriptions.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Y(j\\omega)=X(j\\omega)H(j\\omega)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the operational rule is: if you know the LTI output in the form $x(t)*h(t)$, you can move to frequency analysis by taking the Fourier transform and replacing convolution with multiplication of spectra.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{F}\\{x(t)*h(t)\\}=X(j\\omega)H(j\\omega)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In the next sections, you will use this notation to recognize each side separately and connect the theorem back to LTI output analysis.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Convolution Theorem",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Recognizing the Time-Domain Convolution Side",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $y(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau$, identify the operation and rewrite it in compact convolution notation.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the standard convolution pattern: one factor is written as $x(\\tau)$, the other is shifted to $h(t-\\tau)$, and the whole expression is integrated over $\\tau$ to produce $y(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That pattern is the time-domain side of the theorem, so the operation is convolution. In compact notation, rewrite the output as $y(t)=x(t)*h(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=x(t)*h(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the notation the rest of the topic will reuse whenever an LTI output is being described in the time domain.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Convolution Theorem",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Stating the Matching Fourier-Domain Product",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "If $y(t)=x(t)*h(t)$, write the Fourier-domain expression for the output spectrum.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the time-domain output written in convolution form as $y(t)=x(t)*h(t)$. That tells you to apply the convolution theorem to the output, not to introduce a new operation.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=x(t)*h(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "By the theorem, the Fourier-domain version of that same LTI output is the product of the input and impulse-response spectra. Keep the transformed output label on the left and the two spectra on the right.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Y(j\\omega)=X(j\\omega)H(j\\omega)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the matching frequency-domain statement is $Y(j\\omega)=X(j\\omega)H(j\\omega)$, which is the form the rest of the topic will use when it shifts from time-domain convolution to frequency-domain analysis.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Convolution Theorem",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Interpreting the Theorem as an LTI Output Relationship",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For an LTI system with input $x(t)$ and impulse response $h(t)$, explain what the theorem says about the output in the time and frequency domains.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is one LTI system described by its input $x(t)$ and impulse response $h(t)$. The theorem says to describe that same output in both the time domain and the frequency domain.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned} y(t)&=x(t)*h(t) \\\\ Y(j\\omega)&=X(j\\omega)H(j\\omega) \\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Those two lines are not two different outputs. The first line is the time-domain view, where the output is obtained by convolution, and the second line is the frequency-domain view, where the output spectrum is the product of the input and impulse-response spectra.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the theorem is an output relationship: use the time-domain form when direct output computation is the goal, and use the frequency-domain form when spectral analysis is the clearer way to read the same system.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
